# systemd unit for the vike-datahub data daemon — the market-data recorder and the compute-to-data
# wire in ONE process (ruling 10), in the ONE-PROJECT-FOLDER shape. See docs/ops/datahub-the CI box.md
# for the serving runbook and docs/ops/recorder-deploy.md for the recording one.
#
# ⚠ EVERY PATH BELOW IS THE PLACEHOLDER PROJECT ROOT /srv/vike-<unit>. It is an example, not a
# requirement — the daemon works from ANY absolute path, and THE ROOT IS THE PARAMETER THIS FILE IS
# SUBSTITUTED FOR. Choose your own by ONE rule: a single directory on a disk with room, owned by the
# user the service runs as, holding bin/, settings/ (with state/ and state/logs/) and the tape
# directory named in ReadWritePaths= below. Then substitute it everywhere in the installed copy and
# check nothing was missed:
#
#   R=/your/project/root
#   sed "s,/srv/vike-<unit>,$R,g" deploy/vike-datahub.service > /tmp/vike-datahub.service
#   grep -n /srv/vike-<unit> /tmp/vike-datahub.service    # must print NOTHING
#   sudo install -Dm644 /tmp/vike-datahub.service /etc/systemd/system/vike-datahub.service
#   sudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo systemctl enable --now vike-datahub
#
# A SYSTEM install also wants `User=`/`Group=` added and `WantedBy=` changed to `multi-user.target`
# (this file ships the `--user`-friendly `default.target`); for a --user install put it in
# ~/.config/systemd/user/ instead and drop the sudo. ⚠ Read the hardening block at the bottom
# first: under a --user manager the sandbox directives are applied as NO-OPS on a stock Ubuntu box
# — measured, docs/ops/tradehub-the CI box.md.
#
# ⚠⚠ **ONE UNIT PER DAEMON, AND THIS IS THE DATA DAEMON'S. IT RECORDS.** Until 2026-09-16 this
# directory carried THREE files for this one daemon — this one (serve only), a `-record` twin and a
# `-project` twin with the CI box's real paths filled in — and all three installed under this file's
# name. The owner ruled that shape out: a daemon has ONE unit file, named without a suffix, and the
# PROJECT ROOT is a substitutable parameter rather than a second file. What the three files used to
# argue is folded into this one, and the arguments are kept rather than the files:
#
#   * `-project` (the CI box's real paths) is GONE. The `sed` recipe at the top of this file is what
#     produced it, so a tracked copy of its output was a second spelling of one fact — and the two
#     had already drifted far enough that `crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs` carried
#     named exemptions for them. WHAT PROD2 SUBSTITUTES AND ADDS is written down below rather than
#     shipped as a file; `docs/ops/datahub-the CI box.md` is the operator authority for that box.
#   * `-record` is FOLDED IN, and the fold PICKED RECORDING. See the next block.
#
# ⚠⚠ **WHY THIS FILE RECORDS, AND HOW TO MAKE IT SERVE ONLY.** Ruling 10
# (`docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-09-datahub-market-data-wire-design.md` §0.5) merged
# `vike-recorder` into this daemon so one process owns venue connections, the store and serving.
# That produced TWO DEPLOYMENT SHAPES of one binary, and they were two tracked files because THREE
# directives move together between them — the `--record` flag, `VIKE_DATAHUB_STORE`, and the
# store's line in `ReadWritePaths=`. An operator who moves two of three gets a daemon broken in a
# way systemd cannot see: forget the flag and you get a daemon that is ACTIVE, healthy, answers
# every query and records NOTHING. That install shipped, out of this repository's own runbook.
#
# The collapse had to pick a side, and it picked the RECORDING shape for three reasons:
#
#   1. it is what the only real deployment runs. the CI box's installed `vike-datahub.service` records;
#      a tracked file of that name saying SERVE ONLY is the "install the wrong one" failure above
#      wearing the other face.
#   2. the MEASURED failure is in the serve-only direction. Nobody has ever reported a box that
#      recorded when it should not have; the repository's own runbook shipped one that did not
#      record when it should have.
#   3. since 2026-09-16 a recording start REFUSES when its profile is missing or unusable, before
#      the listener binds (`crates/vike-datahub/src/recorder.rs`'s `load_and_check_profile`). So
#      the shipped default cannot quietly record something nobody asked for: it either records the
#      profile you seeded or it does not start.
#
# TO SERVE ONLY — a box that answers from a store some OTHER process fills — remove all three
# together, and nothing else:
#
#   1. drop the recording flag from `ExecStart=` — `--recorder-profile <name>` as this file ships
#      it, or `--record <path>` on a box using the file spelling;
#   2. leave `VIKE_DATAHUB_STORE=` pointing at the store you READ (it may be outside this root —
#      reads are unaffected by `ProtectSystem=strict`);
#   3. drop the store's entry from `ReadWritePaths=`. ⚠ This one is not tidiness: it is the second
#      leg of the key-less read-only posture
#      (`docs/decisions/0050-a-key-less-datahub-serves-no-delete-verb.md`), and it is a
#      status=226 failure for a box whose store lives outside its own root.
#
# `crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs`'s `a_units_store_grant_follows_its_recording_mode`
# judges whichever shape this file is in, in BOTH directions — it is what stops an edit that moves
# two of the three. It reads this ONE file now rather than holding two apart; the coupling it
# enforces is unchanged, and the floor test beside it (`the_recording_mode_reader_can_actually_fail`)
# is what proves the reader still answers both ways with only one shape in the tree.
#
# ⚠ A `vike-recorder.service` left enabled beside this one is a second writer on the same
# store whose ExecStart names a verb the shipped binary no longer has — see
# `docs/ops/recorder-deploy.md`'s THE MERGE FLIP for the order that retires it without a gap.
# ⚠ /opt/vike is GONE. Binary, settings, state, log and tape now live under ONE project root —
# nothing in /opt, nothing in /var/log — because the settings walk and the log destination both key
# off it.
#
# ⚠⚠ **WHAT PROD2 SUBSTITUTES, AND WHAT IT ADDS.** Kept here because the file that used to carry it
# is gone and because every line of it is a decision somebody made once. MEASURED 2026-09-16.
#
#   the root        `/srv/vike-<unit>` -> the box's own project folder, SHARED with the trading
#                   daemon. One folder, two daemons, each with its own write grant.
#   the store       `market_data/hist` -> `data/hist`. A DECLARED DEPARTURE from the authoritative
#                   spelling (`crates/vike-model/src/state_path.rs`'s PROJECT_DATA_DIR +
#                   HIST_SUBDIR). That box predates the spelling and holds the live tape under
#                   `data/`; its recorder profile names the same directory, so `one_store_root`
#                   passes. Reconciling it is an `mv` of the WHOLE TAPE plus a stop/start of a live
#                   recorder — a scheduled operation, never a side effect of a PR. ⚠ This line
#                   carried "26 GB" twice until 2026-09-20; a live recorder had grown it to 34.4 GiB
#                   and nothing here could be told. `docs/ops/datahub-the CI box.md` is the authority and
#                   now says to ask the box rather than read a number. Consequence on
#                   that box: `vike-cli data ls` with no `--store` resolves the WRONG directory, so
#                   ALWAYS pass `--store` there. `docs/ops/datahub-the CI box.md` is where this lives now.
#   User=/Group=    added. Deliberately NOT shipped here: this file is published to the public
#                   source mirror, whose redaction rewrites a box's user name to a phrase that is
#                   not a legal systemd user name — so a `User=` line here would publish three
#                   units nobody can install. ⚠ ADD YOURS IN THE INSTALLED COPY: a SYSTEM unit with
#                   no `User=` runs as ROOT, which hands root the write grant at the bottom of this
#                   file — and on a shared root that grant sits beside a live order-signing
#                   daemon's tree. The sandbox directives are not a substitute for an unprivileged
#                   identity: they narrow what the process may reach, not who it is.
#   WantedBy=       `default.target` -> `multi-user.target`. This file ships the `--user`-friendly
#                   spelling; a SYSTEM install wants `multi-user.target`.
#   EnvironmentFile= commented OUT. See its own block below for why a shared root makes that the
#                   careful answer rather than a tidy one.
#
# The shape is MACHINE-CHECKED: crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs reads every unit in this
# directory and fails if one names a second location, spreads its write grant over more than one
# ReadWritePaths= LINE, grants a path that covers a binary it runs or its own settings directory,
# sets VIKE_LOG_DIR, points EnvironmentFile= at the credential store, or runs an ExecStartPre= binary
# from outside the project folder (or behind a `-`, which would make the pre-check non-fatal).
#
# The server runs NEXT TO the data and returns compact answers (a BacktestReport), never raw slices.
# A laptop reaches it over an SSH tunnel: ssh -L 7878:localhost:7878 <box>.

[Unit]
Description=vike-datahub — data daemon, RECORD + SERVE: venue feeds fill the hist store it answers from (bind defaults to localhost; <project>/.env wins)
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=simple

# The DataFusion-backed server bin. ⚠ **THE RELEASE SHIPS ONE BACKEND BINARY, `vike-backend`, AND
# `datahub` IS A TOOL INSIDE IT** — since 2026-09-09 the release attaches the multicall and
# `vike-cli` and nothing else, because the multicall already contained every per-tool binary and
# shipping both shipped the same program twice (629 MB against 148 MB, measured on v0.1.20). That is
# why ExecStart below reads `bin/vike-backend datahub`. Dispatch is on a SUBCOMMAND — no symlink, so
# no `203/EXEC` class to inherit from a dangling one.
# ⚠ THE VERB LOST ITS `vike-` PREFIX WHEN THE BINARY WAS RENAMED `vike` -> `vike-backend`, and the
# two halves move together or not at all: `crates/vike/src/lib.rs`'s `resolve` matches a tool by
# EXACT string with no aliases, so `bin/vike-backend vike-datahub` and `bin/vike datahub` are both
# exit 2 with a tool list — a server that "started" and did nothing, not a crash. This line used to
# read `bin/vike vike-datahub` and the claim beside it was that the tool name IS the full binary
# name; that measurement is now history, and its modern form is the sentence above.
# The multicall is built `--features full`, which forwards `vike-datahub/serve-datafusion`, so the
# concrete backend is in.
# Building it yourself, either shape:
#   cargo build --release -p vike-backend --bin vike-backend --features full     # the shipped one
#   cargo build --release -p vike-datahub --features serve-datafusion            # this tool alone
#
# ⚠ THE UPGRADE IS TWO ACTS AND NOTHING SEQUENCES THEM. `deploy/sbin/vike-trader-ci-deploy` installs
# the renamed binary unattended on a tag; THIS file only reaches the box when an operator runs the
# `install -Dm644` recipe above and `systemctl daemon-reload`. For one cycle the helper installed
# `bin/vike-backend` BESIDE `bin/vike` rather than over it, so an un-flipped unit kept starting —
# on the OLD version — while this line was flipped, daemon-reloaded and RESTARTED by hand (the
# helper restarts only vike-tradehub.service). That cycle closed on v0.1.23: `bin/vike` is a
# RETIRED name the next deploy unlinks, so a unit still naming it is 203/EXEC at its next restart.
#
# WorkingDirectory IS the project root: the settings walk and the rolling log resolve off it with no
# extra variable (crates/vike-model/src/state_path.rs's `project_state_dir`).
WorkingDirectory=/srv/vike-<unit>
#
# --- WHERE THE RECORDING PROFILE COMES FROM ------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠⚠ **THE FLAG WAS RENAMED ON 2026-09-22: `--record-profile` IS NOW `--recorder-profile`, AND THE
# OLD SPELLING IS REFUSED BY NAME.** A box whose installed unit still carries the old flag will not
# start after the next tag — it exits 2 with a message naming the new spelling, before the listener
# binds. That is deliberate rather than an oversight: a second accepted spelling is a second name
# that rots, and this daemon's whole refusal posture is that a configuration it cannot honour is a
# stop rather than a degraded start. ⚠ **THE INSTALLED UNIT IS AN OPERATOR ARTIFACT AND THE DEPLOY
# HELPER DOES NOT TOUCH IT** (`deploy/sbin/vike-trader-ci-deploy` replaces the BINARY), so this is
# an edit somebody makes by hand, once, on each box — the same sequencing the `--record` paragraph
# below describes for the 2026-09-16 change.
#
# The object was already called `recorder` in five of six places — `ProfileKind::Recorder`,
# `<project>/settings/recorder.toml`, `vike-cli config recorder`, `vike-cli config mirror
# --recorder` — and this flag was the last holdout. `record` survives as the IMPERATIVE VERB in
# `vike-cli data realtime record add`, which is a different part of speech.
#
# ⚠⚠ **THE VALUE IS OPTIONAL, AND THIS TEMPLATE STILL SPELLS A NAME ON PURPOSE.**
# `--recorder-profile` with no value records whichever recorder profile is ACTIVE (ruling 5 of
# `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-22-data-realtime-record-design.md` — the same row `vike-cli` calls
# the default, resolved by one shared function so the two sides cannot disagree). MEASURED
# 2026-09-22: **nothing in this tree sets an active recorder row.**
# `vike_secrets::profile_store::set_active` has no production caller, and
# `crates/vike-cli/src/cmd/config_mirror_recorder.rs` consults `plan_active_row` with no in-force
# selection, which withholds — it PRESERVES an active row and never creates one. So a migrated box
# holding recorder profiles holds no SELECTION, and the valueless flag would refuse to start there.
# Naming `default` is what works today; drop the value once a verb exists that sets the active row.
#
# ⚠ The flag's PRESENCE — either spelling, with or without a value — is also what
# `crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs`'s `records` reads to decide that this unit WRITES
# the store, and therefore must grant it in `ReadWritePaths=`. A unit that dropped the flag but kept
# the grant (or kept the flag and dropped the grant) is red there in both directions.
#
# ⚠⚠ **`--recorder-profile NAME` NAMES A ROW, NOT A FILE, AND THAT IS NEW ON 2026-09-16.** The
# recorder profile lives in `<project>/settings/db/vike.db` as a `recorder` row plus its
# `subscription` rows — the owner's ruling, overturning
# `docs/decisions/0057-the-seven-settings-files-answered-one-at-a-time.md`'s NO. Seed it once from
# an existing file and the file stops being read:
#
#   vike-cli config mirror --recorder <project>/settings/recorder.toml   # --dry-run first
#   vike-cli config recorder                                            # what the store holds
#
# The mirror REFUSES unless rendering the rows back reproduces the file, so a body that is not what
# this box already records cannot be stored — a recorder profile decides which venue FEEDS OPEN.
#
# ⚠ A profile NAME the store does not hold is REFUSED BEFORE THE LISTENER BINDS
# (`crates/vike-datahub/src/recorder.rs`'s `load_and_check_profile_row`), never a daemon that comes
# up healthy and records nothing. That refusal is what makes it safe for this template to ship the
# recording shape at all.
#
# ⚠ **`--record <path>` still works and is NOT deprecated.** The two are SOURCES, never a precedence
# chain, and giving both is refused rather than resolved. Keep the file spelling when:
#   * this box has not been migrated (no `settings/db/vike.db` — `vike-cli secrets migrate` is the
#     ONE thing that may create it, and a profile write will not);
#   * you are running the commissioning dry run, which points a THROWAWAY copy of the profile at a
#     temp store so it is not a second writer (docs/ops/recorder-deploy.md step 2).
# ⚠ It is also why the CI box's INSTALLED unit may still say `--record <path>` while this template says
# `--recorder-profile`: the deploy helper replaces the BINARY unattended on a tag, so a release that
# repointed `--record` itself would have taken a live recorder down with an argument its own unit
# still spelled as a path.
ExecStart=/srv/vike-<unit>/bin/vike-backend datahub --recorder-profile default

# --- PRE-FLIGHT: refuse a broken configuration instead of starting degraded ----------------------
# Without this line the server STARTS ANYWAY when its settings tree is broken, silently: a
# `settings/` that was never created makes the project walk answer nothing, and this server then
# runs with no settings at all — the case the block further down describes and which nothing until
# now DETECTED. docs/decisions/0013-degrade-vs-refuse.md is the rule this implements.
#
# `vike-cli config check` resolves the SAME settings directory through the SAME loader
# (crates/vike-config/src/load.rs's `load`) and makes the EXIT CODE the product, so a non-zero
# status fails the unit BEFORE ExecStart runs.
#
# ⚠ It refuses only what a silent difference would MISREPRESENT: a settings file that does not parse
# or carries an unknown/removed key, a REMOVED environment variable (which the EnvironmentFile= below
# can still deliver), and a VIKE_SETTINGS_DIR naming a directory that is not there. An ABSENT
# credential store is NOT a failure — this server needs no venue credentials at all, and absent
# credentials ARE the live gate everywhere else.
#
# ⚠ An UNREADABLE credential store — one that exists and cannot be opened — WARNS here rather than
# refusing, and only becomes a refusal when the process environment ARMS a live venue
# (crates/vike-config/src/arming.rs's `CREDENTIAL_FILE_ARMING_REFUSED`). On this server that second
# case does not arise: it mounts no venue and places no order. The distinction still matters for
# reading the output — "not configured" and "cannot open" are different states
# (crates/vike-secrets/src/store.rs's `SecretsError` exists to keep them apart), and the WARN is how
# this box says which one it is in.
#
# ⚠ Deliberately NOT `--strict`, and NO leading `-`. `--strict` promotes every WARNING to a failure,
# including a 0644 `secrets.env` that crates/vike-secrets/src/store.rs's `PermissionWarning` argues
# must never be a refusal; a leading `-` would make a failed pre-check non-fatal, i.e. delete this
# line while appearing to have it. Run `vike-cli config check --strict` by hand when auditing a box
# — the shipped recipe (docs/ops/datahub-the CI box.md) creates neither a credential store nor the `.env`
# below, so it has no warning to promote; a box that later adds the `.env` without a store will see
# one (crates/vike-secrets/src/store.rs's `LegacyStoreWarning`).
#
# ⚠ IT NEEDS A SECOND BINARY IN bin/, or systemd fails the unit with status=203/EXEC. `vike-cli` is
# DataFusion-free — unlike this server, which is built WITH the DataFusion backend — so it adds a
# small binary and no runtime dependency:
#   cargo build --release -p vike-cli --bin vike-cli
#   install -m755 target/release/vike-cli /srv/vike-<unit>/bin/vike-cli
ExecStartPre=/srv/vike-<unit>/bin/vike-cli config check

# --- localhost bind (item 2 of the runbook) -----------------------------------------------------
# The listener should stay bound to localhost — a laptop reaches it through
# `ssh -L 7878:localhost:7878 <box>`, so an OS-account credential is already required to connect.
# ⚠ This line is a DEFAULT, not a guarantee, and the unit cannot make it one: `EnvironmentFile=`
# below beats every `Environment=` line here. What holds the posture is the BINARY: a non-loopback
# address is REFUSED at startup unless VIKE_DATAHUB_ALLOW_PUBLIC_BIND=1 is ALSO set
# (`crates/vike-datahub/src/server.rs`'s `bind_decision` — the tradehub guard's twin), so a
# `VIKE_DATAHUB_ADDR=0.0.0.0:7878` in the project's `.env` now fails the unit loudly instead of
# exposing an unauthenticated server with no unit edit.
# ⚠ And that opt-in is NECESSARY, not sufficient: the same guard takes this server's key presence
# as a third input, so `VIKE_DATAHUB_ALLOW_PUBLIC_BIND=1` on a box with NO
# VIKE_DATAHUB_OBSERVE_KEY / VIKE_DATAHUB_CONTROL_KEY in the credential store is refused too
# (`BindDecision::RefuseUnauthenticated`). Setting the opt-in to "make it reachable" is the
# workaround `docs/decisions/0025-datahub-remote-posture.md` names and refuses; configure the keys
# instead. Do not restate the bind as a property
# in `Description=` — see the measurement in `crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs`'s
# `no_unit_description_claims_what_an_environment_file_can_falsify`.
Environment=VIKE_DATAHUB_ADDR=127.0.0.1:7878

# --- ⚠ POLYMARKET EGRESS: THIS LINE IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT A TIDY DEFAULT ---------------------------
# **The proxy is ON by default, so ABSENCE of this line is not "no proxy" — it is "dial a SOCKS
# proxy at 127.0.0.1:1080".** `crates/bridges/polymarket/src/egress.rs`'s `proxy_url_with` reads
# POLY_PROXY_ENABLED and falls back with `.unwrap_or(true)`; POLY_PROXY_HOST then defaults to
# `127.0.0.1` and POLY_PROXY_PORT to `1080`. A box running no SOCKS proxy therefore fails every
# polymarket stream with `Connection refused` and records nothing from that venue while looking
# healthy in every other respect. That is not hypothetical: the CI box's 2026-09-10 migration rebuilt
# this unit's environment by hand, lost exactly this line, and polymarket recording failed on the
# first start for that reason alone.
#
# The default is ON because Polymarket is geo-blocked in the regions the dev boxes sit in
# (`proxy_url`'s own doc). A box that reaches Gamma and the CLOB WS directly must OPT OUT, which is
# what this line does. A US-hosted box instead sets `POLY_PROXY_ENABLED=true` and points
# POLY_PROXY_HOST / POLY_PROXY_PORT at an EU egress.
#
# ⚠ Deleting this line to "let a file decide" does not work. `dotenv_proxy_vars` lifts the five
# `POLY_PROXY_*` keys out of `load_workspace_dotenv`, whose walk takes no VIKE_SETTINGS_DIR
# override — so dropping this line makes a market-data daemon's egress depend on a file whose whole
# purpose is live venue keys. The process environment layers OVER that store (`proxy_var_layered`
# reads `std::env::var` first), which is exactly why the answer is pinned here, on the unit, where
# a reviewer sees it.
Environment=POLY_PROXY_ENABLED=false

# --- the LIVE MARKET-DATA plane -------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠ The EXACT string "1" arms it (`crates/vike-datahub/src/datahub_cli.rs`, which compares
# `vars.get("VIKE_DATAHUB_LIVE")` against `Some("1")` — this workspace's switch idiom, not a fuzzy
# truthy parse). Unset, this daemon still records and still answers historical queries, but it
# mounts no market-data hub at all and every live subscription is refused with the message in
# `crates/vike-app-core/src/md_session.rs`'s `NO_MD_PLANE`, which names this variable.
#
# ⚠ It is SET in the shipped unit, and that is a deliberate change from the templates this file
# replaced. A recording daemon already holds the venue sockets; the live plane does not open one
# more, it lets a client subscribe to what is already flowing. Leaving it unset produces a daemon
# that looks entirely healthy and serves no live data, whose only symptom is a client-side refusal
# naming a variable nobody set — the silent-half-armed shape this deployment refuses elsewhere. A
# build does not gate it either (`--features live-feeds` gates no code —
# `crates/vike-datahub-client/src/client.rs`), so this line is the whole switch. Comment it out to
# run historical-only.
Environment=VIKE_DATAHUB_LIVE=1

# --- the hist store root (item 3 of the runbook) ------------------------------------------------
# Point at the store this server should answer from. The one-folder default is
# `<project>/market_data/hist` — the STORE, not the data directory above it
# (crates/vike-model/src/state_path.rs's `HIST_SUBDIR` under `PROJECT_DATA_DIR`, which is the leaf
# every reader's own default resolves to). Point it at the RECORDER's project instead when the tape
# is written there, which is the CI box's shape.
# ⚠ **THIS LINE SAID `<project>/data/hist` UNTIL RULING 10, AND THE COMMENT ABOVE IT DID NOT.**
# `PROJECT_DATA_DIR` is `market_data`, so the prose named one directory and the directive named
# another, in one file. That drift is harmless while only this daemon reads the value — and it
# became a HARD STARTUP REFUSAL the moment the recorder merged in, because
# `crates/vike-datahub/src/recorder.rs`'s `one_store_root` compares this value against the profile's
# `store` key and `crates/vike-recorder/recorder.example.toml` ships `market_data/hist`. THE
# AUTHORITATIVE SPELLING IS `PROJECT_DATA_DIR/HIST_SUBDIR`, i.e. `<project>/market_data/hist`, and
# every shipped artifact derives from it:
# `crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs`'s `every_shipped_store_root_is_one_spelling` reads
# this line, the example profile and the install runbook's `sed` and fails if the three disagree.
# (the CI box is the one DECLARED departure — the WHAT PROD2 SUBSTITUTES block in this header carries
# the measurement, and `docs/ops/datahub-the CI box.md` is the operator authority for it.)
# ⚠ It named `<project>/data` with no leaf at all until 2026-08-21 — a root one level ABOVE the
# store, so a box taking the shipped default read a directory holding no `kind=` partitions and
# answered every query with zero rows, indistinguishable from a recorder that found nothing. Keep
# the `hist` leaf.
# Falls back to VIKE_HIST_STORE, then a default, if unset — set it explicitly so a systemd run is
# unambiguous.
#
# ⚠⚠ **THIS VALUE AND THE RECORDING PROFILE'S `store` KEY MUST NAME THE SAME DIRECTORY, AND THE
# DAEMON REFUSES TO START IF THEY DO NOT** (`crates/vike-datahub/src/recorder.rs`'s
# `one_store_root`, called once BEFORE the listener binds and again against the store handle that
# was actually opened). One process owns venue connections, the store and serving, so a recording
# landing in a root the wire does not answer from would be tape nobody can read. The two sides are
# independently authored — one is this systemd line, the other is the profile — which is exactly
# why the refusal exists rather than letting either side silently win.
#
# ⚠ **THE READ-ONLY POSTURE THIS BLOCK USED TO CLAIM DOES NOT APPLY TO A RECORDING UNIT, AND
# SAYING SO IS THE POINT.** `crates/vike-datahub/src/server.rs`'s `delete_series_verb` gives the
# wire a DeleteSeries verb that REMOVES series through `crates/vike-data/src/removal.rs`'s
# `execute_removal`, in EVERY build, behind no feature. Two legs used to stop it here
# (`docs/decisions/0050-a-key-less-datahub-serves-no-delete-verb.md`):
#   * the verb is served only by a KEYED server, and the shipped install configures no
#     VIKE_DATAHUB_OBSERVE_KEY / VIKE_DATAHUB_CONTROL_KEY — so a delete is refused at the verb,
#     before the store is touched. This leg holds under BOTH systemd managers: it is the binary
#     refusing, not the sandbox. **It is the only leg a recording box has.**
#   * the write grant. GONE for this shape by construction — a recorder WRITES the store, so
#     `ReadWritePaths=` must cover it (see the grant block at the bottom). A serve-only edit gets
#     that leg back, and that is one of the three things the SERVE ONLY recipe in the header moves.
# So on a RECORDING box, configuring node keys is the whole distance between the wire and
# `execute_removal`. Configure them deliberately, and read 0050 before you do.
Environment=VIKE_DATAHUB_STORE=/srv/vike-<unit>/market_data/hist

# --- turn the trace FILE log DOWN (item 1 of the runbook) ---------------------------------------
# RUST_LOG/VIKE_LOG filter the CONSOLE only; the file layer defaults to `trace` and captures the
# log->tracing bridge from DataFusion/arrow. A long backfill once wrote 341 GB and nearly filled the
# disk hosting a live trading node — and the CI box runs a live hl-node, so this is not hypothetical.
Environment=VIKE_LOG_FILE_LEVEL=warn
# ⚠ Deliberately NO VIKE_LOG_DIR. Unset, the project layer resolves the rolling log to
# <project>/settings/state/logs — the FIRST path of the write grant at the bottom, and the reason
# that path is granted at all. Naming a directory here would put the log where the grant does not
# reach. Watch it with
# `du -sh /srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state/logs`.

# --- WHERE SETTINGS AND CREDENTIALS LIVE ---------------------------------------------------------
# `<project>/settings/` holds policy.toml, config.toml, preferences.toml, flags.toml, secrets.env
# and state/. The binary finds it by walking UP from its working directory for a project marker: a
# checkout's `Cargo.toml` declaring `[workspace]`, else the NEAREST marker of either kind — for a
# DEPLOYMENT, its own `settings/` directory.
#
# ⚠ A DEPLOYED box has a BINARY, not a source checkout, so until the `settings/` marker existed the
# walk returned NOTHING here and this server started with no settings at all. Create the directory:
#   install -d -m700 /srv/vike-<unit>/settings /srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state
#
# The line below makes the answer EXPLICIT and independent of WorkingDirectory — set it and no walk
# happens at all.
#
# ⚠⚠ **THE NODE KEYS MOVE BEFORE THIS LINE DOES — ALWAYS, IN EITHER DIRECTION.**
# `crates/vike-secrets/src/store.rs`'s `resolve_node_keys` reads VIKE_DATAHUB_OBSERVE_KEY /
# VIKE_DATAHUB_CONTROL_KEY out of `<this directory>/node.env` and out of nowhere else
# (`docs/decisions/0051-node-keys-live-in-their-own-store.md`), so repointing this line at a
# settings tree those keys are not in leaves the daemon KEY-LESS — and SILENTLY, on a loopback
# bind. Nothing fails to start; clients simply stop being able to authenticate. On a SHARED root
# the two families are disjoint (`VIKE_TRADEHUB_*` / `VIKE_DATAHUB_*`), so one `node.env` carries
# both daemons' pairs. Move the keys FIRST, then the line. This warning is repeated at the line
# where the mistake would be typed on purpose.
Environment=VIKE_SETTINGS_DIR=/srv/vike-<unit>/settings

# Operator tunables (NOT credentials) from a plain env file at the PROJECT ROOT. The datahub server
# itself needs no venue creds; harmless if the file is absent.
#
# ⚠ It MUST NOT be settings/secrets.env. The credential store is deliberately NEVER an environment
# source — four gates in this workspace (VIKE_TRADEHUB_LIVE, VIKE_TRADEHUB_CONTROL, VIKE_RECONCILE,
# VIKE_TELEGRAM_CONTROL) are read off the REAL process env precisely BECAUSE the store never reaches
# there (crates/vike-ops/src/reconcile_config.rs's module doc is the authority), and an
# EnvironmentFile= at the store both inverts that and exports every key into /proc/<pid>/environ.
#
# ⚠⚠ **ON A SHARED ROOT THIS DIRECTIVE REACHES BOTH DAEMONS, AND IT WINS OVER EVERY `Environment=`
# LINE IN THIS FILE — IN BOTH ORDERS** (measured on the CI box, systemd 255; see
# `crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs`'s
# `no_unit_description_claims_what_an_environment_file_can_falsify`). One line in the trading
# daemon's operator file would then reach this daemon's bind address, its store root and its live
# market-data plane. the CI box therefore COMMENTS THIS DIRECTIVE OUT rather than repointing it, and the
# `Environment=` lines above become the final answer. That is the careful move, not a tidy one: the
# directive it replaced named a file that had never existed, and repointing it at the surviving
# root would have handed this daemon the TRADING daemon's `.env` — a BEHAVIOUR change riding
# invisibly on a LAYOUT change, discovered at the next restart.
#
# ⚠ A daemon that wants one on a shared root needs its OWN file, named here. The layout gate
# requires `<project>/.env` exactly, so a second operator file under one root is not expressible
# today; that is a real constraint and the reason the CI box comments rather than re-plumbs.
#
# ⚠ A silent-series pager's credentials MAY go here — they are pager credentials, not venue keys,
# and `webhook_targets` in `crates/vike-datahub/src/recorder.rs` layers the process environment
# OVER the store, so either channel works:
#   VIKE_ALERT_TELEGRAM_TOKEN=...  +  VIKE_ALERT_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=...  ->  the "telegram" target
#   VIKE_ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=...                                        ->  the "webhook" target
# and the profile's `[alerting].webhooks` names which of them a silent-series alert is sent to.
# Absent both, the alert still FIRES and still reaches this unit's log.
EnvironmentFile=-/srv/vike-<unit>/.env

# --- keep a runaway sweep from starving the live node (item 3 of the runbook) -------------------
# A concurrent sweep is an N_threads x slice MEMORY multiplier on a box that also hosts live trading.
# Cap it so the server is OOM-killed before it can starve the hl-node; tune to the box's headroom.
MemoryMax=8G
# Restart on a crash (an OOM kill included), but not on a clean exit.
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5

# --- the graceful stop the RECORDING plane owes an operator -------------------------------------
# SIGTERM raises the same stop flag the interactive `quit` word raises, and the teardown then runs:
# unsubscribe every feed, FLUSH THE WRITER (this is the tape), report dropped rows, join compaction.
# One stop flag, many triggers; the decision, the the CI box measurements and the options that were
# refused are in docs/ops/graceful-stop.md, and docs/ops/recorder-deploy.md quantifies what a
# SIGKILL mid-flush costs in rows.
#
# ⚠ **THE BUDGET IS DERIVED AND THIS IS THE UNIT THE DERIVATION READS.** With `--record` the process
# holds BUFFERED PARQUET ROWS, and `TimeoutStopSec=` is the only thing between the final flush and a
# SIGKILL. It must exceed `crates/vike-datahub/src/recorder.rs`'s `TOTAL_STOP_BUDGET_SECS` (the feed
# unsubscribe PLUS the bounded flush/compaction tail), which that file's
# `the_whole_stop_fits_inside_the_units_stop_timeout` reads out of THIS file and asserts. It was 30
# on the retired `deploy/vike-recorder.service` while that test compared only the capped TAIL against
# it, so the advertised budget excluded the largest step — do not lower it without moving the test.
#
# ⚠ **A SERVE-ONLY START HAS NO TEARDOWN AT ALL, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE RATHER THAN AN OMISSION.**
# `crates/vike-datahub/src/recorder.rs`'s `arm` installs the signal handler only when `--record` was
# given, because a handler that raises a flag nothing reads would take SIGTERM's DEFAULT KILL away
# from a daemon with nothing to flush — a data server that stopped answering `systemctl stop` would
# be a worse regression than anything the merge fixed. So an edit that drops the recording flag
# (either spelling — `--record` or `--recorder-profile`) also drops
# the derivation above: the number below stays only because every shipped unit must STATE its stop
# bound rather than inherit systemd's 90 s default
# (`crates/vike-ops/tests/graceful_stop_pin.rs`), and because a unit that says nothing about its
# stop is a number nobody chose.
#
# ⚠ **`VIKE_DATAHUB_LIVE=1` IS THE ONE CASE THAT COULD HAVE CHANGED THAT AND DOES NOT.** A
# live-market-data start DOES grow threads — one publish thread, one reconciler, and a venue feed
# per subscribed key — so the obvious reading is that the serve-only shape would owe a teardown
# after all. It does not, and the argument is the same one `arm` makes:
# `crates/vike-datahub/src/server.rs`'s `serve_authed` is a blocking
# `for incoming in listener.incoming()` loop that polls NO stop flag, so a handler installed for it
# would raise a flag nothing reads — `systemctl stop` would hang for the full TimeoutStopSec= below
# and then SIGKILL, which is strictly WORSE than SIGTERM's default kill. And the md plane has
# nothing to lose to one: the hub holds book slots, a bounded in-memory tape and per-connection
# mailboxes, it writes no store, and a dropped venue socket is reaped by the venue. A SIGKILL costs
# a subscriber a reconnect, not data.
# ⚠ The dependency worth writing down for the next reader: that argument rests on the accept loop
# polling no flag. If a future change gives it one (the MAX_CONNECTIONS/ConnSlot guard touches that
# same loop), the serve-only shape's TimeoutStopSec= becomes a REAL budget that nobody has derived,
# and the derivation belongs beside `crates/vike-datahub/src/recorder.rs`'s FEED_STOP_BUDGET_SECS.
KillSignal=SIGTERM
TimeoutStopSec=45

# Hardening: the server needs only to read the store and bind localhost.
NoNewPrivileges=true
# ⚠ ProtectSystem=strict mounts the WHOLE filesystem read-only except /dev, /proc, /sys and whatever
# ReadWritePaths= names. READS are unaffected, so the settings above and the hist store both load
# normally — which is why a store OUTSIDE the project root needs no entry here.
#
# ⚠ MEASURED on the CI box (systemd 255, Ubuntu 24.04): under a `systemd --user` manager with the
# distribution default `kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=1`, these directives are applied
# as NO-OPS — no warning, no failed unit, and `systemctl --user show` still reports them as
# configured. They enforce under the SYSTEM manager. docs/ops/tradehub-the CI box.md has the probe.
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=yes
PrivateTmp=true
# ONE grant, and deliberately the SUB-directory rather than the project root: this server writes its
# rolling log and nothing else, so granting the root would hand it write access to the store it is
# meant to read and to the binary it is running. Anything writable by this unit must be data, never
# code. ⚠ The path must EXIST, and the install recipe must create it: `write_path`'s lazy
# `create_dir_all` cannot make it under a read-only parent. Under the SYSTEM manager systemd also
# refuses to start a unit whose ReadWritePaths= names a missing path (status=226); under a --user
# manager it does NOT — the same root cause as the sandbox no-op measured on the CI box, whose probe is
# in docs/ops/tradehub-the CI box.md. Do not read that refusal as a safety net on a --user install.
#
# ...and deliberately NO Environment=VIKE_HALT_FILE either — the mirror-image reason. The tradehub
# unit sets it for explicitness, but the HALT sentinel is checked at the venue-adapter SUBMIT
# boundary and this server has no execution path to check it: it links no bridge crate and holds no
# `ExecutionClient`. A VIKE_HALT_FILE line here would name a file nothing in this process ever
# reads — worse than absent, because an operator who touches it during an incident believes something
# stopped. Nothing did, and nothing needed to: this server places no orders and holds no trading
# credentials. See docs/ops/kill-switches.md §G.
# ⚠⚠ **TWO PATHS, AND BOTH ARE LOAD-BEARING FOR A RECORDING UNIT.** `settings/state/logs` is where
# the rolling trace file goes; `market_data` is the directory the tape is written under. Without
# the first the daemon cannot write its own log; without the second it starts, brings the wire up,
# subscribes, buffers and then dies at the FIRST Parquet flush — with `systemctl status` reading
# active for as long as a buffer takes to fill.
#
# ⚠ It is the store's PARENT that is granted, not the store leaf. The leaf is created by the first
# flush, and systemd refuses to start a unit whose `ReadWritePaths=` names a path that does not
# exist (status=226). Granting the parent is also what lets an operator move the tape between
# sub-directories without editing this line: the CI box's 2026-08-21 rename of `data/tape` to
# `data/hist` was an ordinary `mv` for exactly that reason. The profile's `store =` MUST be inside
# the granted directory; a store outside it fails the daemon at open, which is at least loud.
#
# ⚠⚠ **`settings/state/LOGS`, NOT `settings/state` — AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A SAFETY FINDING, NOT
# TIDINESS.** An earlier version of this grant named `settings/state` and called that the narrowest
# possible grant. Both halves were wrong the moment two daemons shared one project root, which is
# what the one-folder shape means: `settings/state` is then the LIVE TRADING DAEMON's directory
# too, and granting it hands a market-data recorder write access to
#
#   settings/state/HALT            the kill-switch sentinel (`crates/vike-bridge-core/src/halt.rs`
#                                  rung 2) — writable means DELETABLE after somebody touches it
#   settings/state/changes/        the append-only operator-command journal, which
#                                  `docs/decisions/0054-settings-move-into-one-database.md` keeps as
#                                  a FILE deliberately
#   settings/state/LIVE-*.lock     the sentinels that stop two live processes trading one account
#   settings/state/strategy-state/ per-mount state the core writes
#
# The stated reason for accepting the wide grant — "settings/state is the only directory a
# project's own rolling log can go to" — is false. The log goes to `settings/state/logs`, and
# granting only that subdirectory is enough. MEASURED: with `settings/state/logs` here the whole of
# `crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs` passes, so the narrower grant costs no gate change
# and was available the whole time. MEASURED inside the daemon's own mount namespace on the CI box: with
# it, `settings/state`, `changes`, `LIVE-bybit.lock` and `strategy-state` are all REFUSED while
# `settings/state/logs` and the tape directory are writable.
#
# The lesson is not that somebody was careless. It is that **a grant's SAFETY is a property of the
# NEIGHBOUR, not of the path string** — the identical line was correct while this daemon owned its
# own root and wrong the moment it shared one, with no character changed. Anybody repointing a
# `WorkingDirectory=` re-reads every `ReadWritePaths=` in the same edit and asks whose directory
# each one now names.
#
# ⚠ TO SERVE ONLY, drop the store's entry from this line — and drop the recording flag
# (`--recorder-profile` as shipped, or `--record` on a box using the file spelling) and leave
# `VIKE_DATAHUB_STORE=` alone in the same edit. A serve-only start only READS the store, and reads
# are unaffected by `ProtectSystem=strict`, so keeping the grant is not merely redundant: it
# removes the second leg of the key-less read-only posture
# (`docs/decisions/0050-a-key-less-datahub-serves-no-delete-verb.md` — with node keys configured it
# is the ONLY thing left in front of `crates/vike-data/src/removal.rs`'s `execute_removal`), and it
# is a status=226 failure for a box whose store lives outside its own root.
# `crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs`'s `a_units_store_grant_follows_its_recording_mode`
# judges this file in whichever shape it is in, in both directions.
# ⚠ ONE LINE if it names two paths: deploy_layout_gate.rs fails a unit that spreads its grant
# over more than one ReadWritePaths= line, because a second line is where a grant nobody re-read
# gets added.
# ⚠ NOT the project root. That would cover bin/ (the binary this unit executes) and settings/ (the
# credential store, the armed notional ceiling), which the same gate refuses — a data daemon must
# not be able to rewrite the program it runs, its neighbour's cap or its neighbour's keys.
ReadWritePaths=/srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state/logs /srv/vike-<unit>/market_data


# --- syscall / capability hardening, MEASURED ON THE REAL BINARIES -------------------------------
# ⚠ MEASURED on the CI box 2026-08-17, systemd 255, with throwaway SYSTEM units running these exact
# binaries — the set below is not a template copied from a hardening guide, every line survived a
# run. The recorder's own `--once` dry run exits 0 under it, and that run dials 8 live polymarket
# streams and 2 binance streams, so the TLS handshakes and WebSocket dials happen UNDER the seccomp
# filter; vike-tradehub reaches `{"kind":"ready"}`. `systemd-analyze security`: recorder 8.7 -> 1.9,
# tradehub 8.3 -> 1.7. Before this block the units carried the four filesystem directives above and
# NONE of these — the whole syscall and capability surface was open.
#
# Nothing here is a guess about what the daemon needs: it runs as an ordinary user, acquires no
# capability, creates no namespace of its own, takes no realtime priority and executes one ABI.
#
# ⚠ PrivateNetwork= is deliberately ABSENT and always will be — these daemons ARE network clients.
# `systemd-analyze` charges 0.5 for that plus 0.3 for holding AF_INET sockets at all; both are
# unrecoverable for anything that talks to a venue, and adding it to chase the number would produce a
# daemon that cannot do its job. RestrictAddressFamilies= therefore ALLOWS the three families in use
# rather than pretending the internet is optional.
#
# ⚠ The one line with a real blast radius is UMask=0077: new files become owner-only. Checked on
# the CI box before adopting — `lsof +D <project>` shows exactly one user touching the tree, so nothing
# else reads the store today. A future reader under a DIFFERENT account breaks on it, loudly, with a
# permission error rather than silence. That is the intended trade for a tree holding venue keys.
CapabilityBoundingSet=
AmbientCapabilities=
RestrictNamespaces=yes
RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_INET AF_INET6 AF_UNIX
RestrictSUIDSGID=yes
RestrictRealtime=yes
LockPersonality=yes
MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes
SystemCallArchitectures=native
SystemCallFilter=@system-service
SystemCallFilter=~@privileged @resources @obsolete @mount @module @reboot @swap @raw-io @debug @clock @cpu-emulation
ProtectKernelTunables=yes
ProtectKernelModules=yes
ProtectKernelLogs=yes
ProtectControlGroups=yes
ProtectClock=yes
ProtectHostname=yes
ProtectProc=invisible
ProcSubset=pid
UMask=0077
[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
